# One Day in Athens: Lament After Killing the Sage
Tamil title: ஆதென்ஸ் நகரில் அன்றோர் நாள்! அறிஞனை அழித்தபின் அழுகுரல்!

“Meletus!”

“Who is that?”

“Why are you so frightened?”

“Ah! Is it you, Anytus? Come in. I wondered who it was.”

“You were terrified, madman! What is there to fear now? Why fear after victory?”

“Do not mock me, Anytus. Hearing a voice so suddenly startled me a little.”

“Deep in thought?”

“Yes. I was thinking about something. Leave that aside. What are people saying?”

“Saying? Meletus, they are praising us. If we ask, they will pour gold at our feet.”

“I did not ask about the wealthy. How else could they behave? What are the people saying?”

“The crowd cries out and clamours. Some say, ‘Alas, the poor old man troubled no one. Could they kill him?’ Others are afraid to say even that, wondering what needless trouble might come. Will the people ever hold one steady position? Why should we care? Friend, there is joy in the mansions and praise for us. They call us men of extraordinary ability who successfully complete whatever we undertake. Meletus, the rich even possess a secret fear of us.”

“Fear? Of us? Why?”

“Why has your brain gone dry, Meletus? How could they not fear? We brought down even a Socrates. We worked so cleverly and won. Can they avoid fearing what would happen if that ability were directed against them? Henceforth in Greece, what we two lay down is law. All these lords will submit to us. This is the hour for celebration, yet you sit brooding in distress. Come. Friends await us in the banquet hall. Flavourful grape wine, music sweeter still, and dances delightful to the eye, all for us. Come!”

It was true. Some base men swelled with delight upon hearing that the noble man was gone. They treated the day as one of pleasure, feasted, and celebrated.

Meletus too left home thinking it a festival. He wore a silk robe worked with zari and drank grape wine. He spent a little time in the house of a woman offering the pleasures of love, heard music, watched dance, and ate fine fruits from her hand. The beauty made him laugh and praised his bravery; he praised her loveliness. She was happy, but he was not.

There was dizziness and tears. The whole city of Athens seemed to be looking at him and laughing. Meletus tried walking quickly, then slowly; he tried drink and wandered through gardens. Wherever he went and whatever he did, there was no peace. He saw that Socrates had become permanent everywhere and was terrified.

“Shame, what a cowardly heart!” he told himself.

“It will be like this for a day or two. People will talk, then they will forget,” he thought, trying to gain courage.

“The name of the noble will endure as long as the world,” came a voice. A man driving sheep was speaking to another.

As long as the world! The words began stabbing Meletus's heart. He wanted to sit somewhere alone, hidden from everyone, and wail. Socrates! Socrates everywhere, in everyone's thoughts, walking throughout Athens. Socrates appeared in the grief covering an old man's face, the bewilderment of women, the sorrowful gaze of youths, and the sad faces of children. Wherever Meletus looked, he saw Socrates in a form that seemed to laugh and ask, “Madman, what did you gain by killing me?”

Meletus plunged into the river and swam until exhausted. He climbed and descended hills until his legs ached, hoping to tire himself enough to sleep. It did not work. He thought of speaking with Anytus, who appeared and spoke to him, but his mind would not let him remain. Claiming urgent work, he wandered from place to place. Socrates refused to leave and followed, asking mockingly, “Meletus, who won? You who walk alive, or I who lost my life?”

“Socrates is a corpse, while I am the madman, frightening myself by thinking of him,” Meletus reflected.

“What if Socrates died? Where can his power go?” a young man said angrily to someone.

Thinking it dangerous to be seen, Meletus hid like a thief inside an old ruined house.

“Grandmother, how old was he?” a boy asked.

“Seventy, child, seventy years old. Sinners conspired together and killed a man that old,” an old woman replied.

Meletus did not enter the house. Stone and earth lay piled there, and he sat upon them, afraid to go inside.

“Poor old man! What did he do, Grandmother?”

“He taught people to use their reason and denounced those who practised fraud.”

“That is good work, is it not?” the guileless boy asked.

“Can wicked people bear the sight of those doing good? See how beautiful the dove is and how ugly the hawk that strikes and devours it!”

Meletus began thinking he must leave.

“Grandmother, when I grow up, if I find the murderer who killed Grandfather Socrates, I shall certainly kill him,” the boy said furiously.

The old woman kissed him and said, “Child, we need not wait so long. Our people acted hastily, fell into the net of murderous agitators, and became accomplices. Soon clarity will come to their minds. They will understand their mistake and cut into pieces the sinners who devised the plot and throw them to the vultures.”

Meletus quietly departed and again wandered like a madman.

Meanwhile Anytus thought Meletus was drowning in needless grief. The affair had succeeded. Socrates, who had gathered all the vigorous young men to his side and lived like an uncrowned king of Greece, was dead. Why fear? There were wealthy men ready to give gold, support from rulers, and a place in the council. With that man gone, they could become prominent men of Greece. This was the time to rejoice, yet mad Meletus lay trembling.

Anytus walked happily onward. Seeing a slave approach, he asked whether the man's master was home.

“He is,” the slave answered with some indifference.

“What is he doing?”

“Wailing at the sight of his only daughter's life ebbing away,” the slave replied, hurrying off.

A poem about maidens dying when love fails came to Anytus's mind. Thinking his friend's daughter suffered that illness, he went there. Upon arriving, Anytus became Meletus.

The beautiful young woman's head was bleeding. Her pure white dress was drenched in blood. Her eyes were closed. Life had not left, but she was in grave danger.

“My precious daughter! Alas, my precious daughter!” the lord wailed. Servants and friends surrounded him. He did not notice Anytus.

Anytus asked a servant what had happened.

“The young mistress fell from the upper floor.”

“By accident?”

“Deliberately. She decided to die and threw herself down to end her life.”

“Why? A matter of love?”

“Look, sir, you are mad. Love indeed! When she heard Socrates had died, her heart broke. She decided it was wrong to remain alive in a country that had killed such a noble man and tried to destroy herself.”

Those words turned Anytus into Meletus. Had Socrates' death caused so great a shock? A woman treated her life as a straw, declaring that when the noble man died, she would no longer live in the world.

Anytus became afraid even to remain there. He left the mansion, but Socrates began pursuing him.

The people cried, “Injustice! Outrage!” Voices condemned the criminals who supposedly governed but had murdered a political thinker. Hot words named them jealous men, deceivers, and murderous sinners.

“They plotted to kill Socrates. It was the conspiracy of that wretch Meletus and the unworthy Anytus,” said informed voices.

“Puppets of selfish men,” another said contemptuously.

“And we tolerated this outrage?” someone asked.

“We? Logs of wood, dolls of clay, lumps of flesh, chaff among the people, ingrates! Are we human? No, not even beasts. An animal turns upon the animal or hunter who attacks its parents. We are lower than that. A few base men plotted to take the life of our country's peerless sage, and we kept our mouths shut.”

Anytus's fear grew. He had mocked Meletus, but what was his own condition now? He fled the street at a near run and wandered like Meletus. Wherever he went he saw Socrates, and his mind too became confused.

Those who had said before the court that Socrates corrupted Greek youth, ruined ancient traditions, destroyed religion's dignity, and shattered awareness of God, and therefore must be poisoned as a contentious man, had really resented his influence and conspired because their authority could not spread while he lived. After Socrates became a corpse, all of them were seized by great fear.

While he lived, though inwardly afraid, they spoke dismissively to preserve false prestige. Once he calmly drank poison and died, their fear increased a thousandfold. The people's love for Socrates became unmistakable. The conspirators saw citizens angry enough to burn with their gaze everyone responsible for his pitiful end, and they trembled.

They thought his death had become a grave danger to them. Why had they committed this evil? Why had they plotted to destroy one whom the people sincerely called virtuous, wise, learned, and enlightened? Fear and sorrow tormented the conspirators.

Socrates' death had awakened a new force, one possessing more influence than Socrates himself had. By field and canal, in flower garden and market street, playground and gymnasium, houses of poets and painters, bathing places and sculptors' workshops, everywhere stood the ranks of Socrates' army.

“Meletus! Anytus! This murder is the work of those great sinners!” voices cried.

“Seventy years old, he spent his whole life for the people's welfare. He laboured without seeking wealth, comfort, or status. He assembled an army of knowledge for Greece, and the country rewarded him with poison. We poisoned wisdom! Misled by the perverse minds of a few idlers, we made a great scholar a corpse. We destroyed the one who gave knowledge and killed the one who gave love,” the people grieved.

Meletus and Anytus escaped only because the people were immersed in grief; otherwise they would have become corpses that very day. They were left alive for a few days as walking corpses.

They overcame their fear somewhat and assumed their position would improve when the public shock subsided. They were deceived. The matter did not end with shock; the event had entered deeply into people's minds. Sorrow, shame, anger, and an agitation that could not subside until something was done turned the people into a storm.

Whenever they saw Socrates' friends, those who remained with him to the end, argued his innocence, and spoke for him before the court, people felt ashamed. “We refused these scholars' words and were deceived by believing the great sinner Meletus,” they said with melting hearts.

“What happened has happened. What can we do now?” some asked sorrowfully.

“What can we do? The conspirators Meletus and Anytus who dared this outrage...” someone began.

“Yes! They must be killed!” many shouted.

“Meletus! Anytus! Catch them! Strike! Do not let go! Kill!” The cry arose in Athens and rapidly grew in force.

“Socrates was spotless, a man of wisdom. He did not damage religion or lead people astray. Some had corrupted us in religion's name, and Socrates told us that truth. He stirred thought and said our own reason was superior to every borrowed doctrine. His heart was pure and his speech a great feast of knowledge.

“He dragged frauds into the open, confounded those wrapped in religious cloaks, and drove away the ignorant who called themselves sages and deceived people. He showed that the gurus were blind in knowledge. ‘Know yourself! Seek truth! Make your heart an abode of purity! Investigate! Honour knowledge!’ These were his moral teachings. Yet we ignorant and ungrateful people listened to deceivers and killed him with poison.”

Such words arose throughout Athens. The people cried that their anger would not subside until the treacherous Meletus and Anytus were killed.

A great storm arose with a magnitude and speed unknown while Socrates lived. Meletus and Anytus could not escape it.

“Where is the conspirator Meletus?”

The desire for vengeance strengthened, and the demand to destroy the conspirators grew. Public rage fell upon Anytus and Meletus, who had stood before the court, looked contemptuously at the symbol of knowledge, and imposed baseless blame.

“Where are the conspirators? Drag Meletus here! Drag Anytus here!” the people commanded.

“Here is the great sinner!” Some fulfilled the command and dragged Meletus in. Meletus was killed. Anytus was driven from the country.

When the court had said, “You are elderly. We do not wish to sentence you to death. You may request another punishment. Will you leave Athens? We can exile you; do you agree?” Socrates had answered, “People of Athens, if my teaching has aroused hatred in my own town, where and how can I find affection abroad?”

Those wise words must have returned to Anytus as he fled Athens to escape popular anger. What became of him? The fool thought he had escaped death's grasp, but death followed without his knowing.

He reached Heraclea. It too was a town filled with people, and their minds too concluded that the great sinner who conspired to kill spotless Socrates must be destroyed. The residents recognised Anytus.

“The great sinner has come here! The wretch who plotted to kill the noble man labouring to spread truth has come. Anytus has arrived, the killer of the great teacher of virtue!”

People gathered, chased him, and hurled stones. They continued while he screamed, until their anger subsided and he died. The villain Anytus was stoned to death. The petty men who first rejoiced that they had killed Socrates perished as victims of popular fury.

The people had first listened to agitators, reviled spotless Socrates as an enemy of religion, and dared poison the sage. They quickly understood their mistake and sought atonement. They killed the conspirators who had led them astray and resolved never again to abandon Socrates' moral path.

Yes. But did the cruelty of noble people's blood being spilt by the plots of base men cease? No. Blood has continued to flow until today. Why?

Meletus and Anytus envied Socrates. They joined selfish men who feared his influence would destroy their old authority and hereditary privileges, accused him of blasphemy and hostility to God, persuaded the people, and had him poisoned. The people soon understood their error and wailed that they had killed a world-renowned scholar. They killed both evil men. The portion of the article you have read so far explains their fate and the course taken by the public mind.

From time to time, selfish seekers of domination have continued inciting people to kill, exile, and harm noble reformers. They raise a cry:

“People! A grave danger has arisen. An arrogant scoundrel who regards himself as master of every art threatens our ancient religion, traditions, ancestral arrangements, and true faith. This ignorant man shouts that he will destroy everything and establish his authority. He is a schemer seeking to turn people his way in a moment of confusion and a trickster making falsehood look like truth. He wanders speaking of new philosophies. Do not be enchanted. Do not surrender your reason or fall into his net. Do not ruin the faith. There will be no salvation or divine grace. Understand what I say. Suppress the wicked man today!”

Meletus and Anytus in the days of Socrates; Godse in our day.

Between them came countless Judases, accusers, and conspirators.

People yield to such incitement and destroy pure-hearted persons. When they later gain time to think calmly, they understand their mistake, weep, and feel ashamed. Standing with tear-filled eyes, they vow never again to be driven into frenzy by empty words or destroy noble people. Yet world history is full of tragedies showing how often they fail to keep that promise.

Twenty-four hundred years ago, Meletus and Anytus falsely accused Socrates, who taught true knowledge, made people misunderstand him, and destroyed him. Selfish people can still do the same today, despite education and enlightenment spreading widely across the world. Between the age of Meletus and ours, many noble people have fallen victim to the anger of those who lost their reason.

From then until today, the public mind has remained capable of entering frenzy with great ease. Once people understand the disaster caused by that frenzy, they grieve and feel profound shame, seek atonement, blame scoundrels for giving them evil thoughts, and rise to take revenge. This too remains an unchanging public habit.

Why does the public mind stagger so? People suspect and torture the spotless, later recognise their mistake, reform, regret their former action, identify those responsible, and seek revenge. After some time they again begin suspecting innocent people. Thus their condition changes repeatedly.

A volcano at least spews fire only occasionally and causes no danger while quiet. The public mind not only produces a volcano's destructive harm but appears a strange volcano indeed. At one time it pours the fire of hatred, at another the milk of love. We cannot see the flood of affection and rejoice that the world will henceforth prosper, for it later begins spewing fire again.

Because the public intellect is so disastrous and strange a volcano, the blood of noble men like Socrates was shed. Tears then washed away the stain. Once the place was clean, another noble man's blood was shed, and tears again washed the stain. History displays this chain. We need an explanation, for only then can humanity reduce the great periodic loss and suffering. It must do so.

Any doctrine, principle, or practice takes time to enter the public mind; once planted, it takes still longer to dissolve. Wax melts quickly, while iron needs more time to become red-hot. Likewise, heat that has entered red-hot iron cannot be removed rapidly. Though the fire is out and the iron lies outside the furnace, a hand that assumes it cool will be burnt. Pouring cold water upon it to reduce the heat instantly may damage the object.

So ideas take time to enter people's minds, and still more time is required to remove established ideas and admit new ones. Old doctrines within rage and refuse to leave, while new doctrines outside fight with determination to enter. When these forces strike each other, the human descends to an animal state and disasters occur. Afterwards he sobs, falls in worship, and swears tearfully never to do it again, yet repeats the error.

The doorway into the mind is so narrow. That is why a doctrine struggles both to enter and leave. During festivals, does not a third-class carriage on the South Indian Railway turn a gentle man into a ruffian, the compassionate hard-hearted, women into men, and men mad?

The struggle occurs because the mind is not spacious and its entrance does not permit free traffic. So long as the mind retains the form and condition of a cave, this dreadful situation will remain. The mind must become spacious, and its entrance must not be congested. People must continually broaden their minds, because every day new doctrines arrive seeking room. Unless space increases, there will be crowding and a battle at the entrance.

Socrates set several new ideas in motion. They tried to find room in Greek minds, while the thoughts already there refused to yield. That was natural.

We railway passengers behave the same way. Though those inside say, “The carriage will soon be empty. Do not hurry or rage. Let us get down; give us room, and then you can board peacefully,” we answer, “Yes, yes,” while pushing down those alighting, jumping through windows, throwing luggage ahead, suffering inconvenience, and causing it.

After Socrates, whenever scholars arose to introduce new political, social, religious, artistic, or economic ideas, this conflict arose because the mind had not been sufficiently enlarged. When tragedies like the poisoning of Socrates occurred, people stopped with revenge and tearful vows. They did not decide to prevent repetition by continually broadening the mind. They found pleasure in vengeance and forgot the subtle lesson. Treating revenge as the task, they believed their duty complete and did not repair the doorway of the mansion of the mind.

This time, in our own day, we should not use this tragic event for futile attacks or the ancient frenzy of demanding revenge. We should take it as the announcement of the day to begin broadening people's minds.

Our responsibility and need in this matter are greater than those of other peoples. For two centuries our minds were pawned to the white man and have only recently been redeemed. Much has corroded and much repair remains. We must expand the mind and enlarge its entrance. This is the time when new ideas can and must arrive swiftly and abundantly. We must therefore act more skilfully and rapidly than other nations in broadening our minds if we are to avoid conflict within them and the resulting immense losses.

We must admit frankly that while other nations investigate the nature of the moon, we remain at the stage of debating whether Jagannath would be angry or pleased if tribal people entered his temple. That is sufficient proof of how narrow, dark, and troubled the mind remains.

At least on the funeral day of the noble man whose death moves many nations to tears, before the lakhs who gather, let the act dearest to his heart be performed. Great men, open the Jagannath temple at Puri today. Let tribal people enter and their hearts rejoice. Though high leaders appealed in this way, the temple door was reportedly pulled shut and locked by the pandas, the temple priests. Such is the present extent and condition of the mind. New-world ideas must enter this cramped and scorched mind. I say this so that we may understand how difficult the work is.

“I heard that some celebrated the death of the peerless noble man and distributed sweets. I will not even regard them as Indians,” Pandit Jawahar said in Jammu last week.

How did they acquire the heart to rejoice? The minds of some lie so narrow and dark. If the country is to have new life, many new and necessary ideas and methods must be introduced among such people. How can this be achieved?

If fanatical feeling could arise strongly enough to kill Gandhi, who lived without wounding any mind or arousing anyone's jealousy and, as Pandit Jawahar said, stood like a sacred bridge between antiquity and modernity, it shows what dark rooms exist in some minds in our country.

We must change this condition, broaden the mind, and create generous room, opportunity, and ability for old ideas to leave and new ones to enter. That alone truly honours the blood shed. It is not enough to continue the pattern from Socrates' time: base men conspire to spill a noble man's blood, and people later wash away the stain with tears.

Our tears merely help lighten the weight of our sorrow. They alone cannot abolish the frenzy that drinks noble lives. Rays must arise from the eyes of knowledge so that mental darkness departs and the mind expands. Only then can our people attain a good condition admired by the whole world.

“A new interpretation of varnashrama: caste distinctions are not wanted.

“Untouchability is a dreadful disease that destroys religion, society, and human character; therefore it must be abolished entirely.

“A country cannot prosper while the poor weep; ways must be found to bring happiness into their lives.

“We must not distinguish people by religion and race, forget humanity, and create savagery in the country.”

These were the ideas the noble Gandhi introduced.

He did not introduce them harshly or bitterly. Like a mother feeding fruit to a child, he offered them lovingly in small portions. Yet an intention to take his life arose in this country.

What remains to be done here is no small amount. Ask the envoys who have visited various countries; they will describe the immense changes and new ideas this country requires. All must be introduced. A way must be created, and the mind must broaden.

Displaying sorrow and anger serves no purpose. This is the time to show the courage to perform the great work of broadening the mind. We must not be known only for shedding tears. We must understand our duty and show that we know how, and are able, to enlarge the mind.

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 22 April 1948.

---NOTES---
The translation preserves both canonical OCR parts as one continuous article: all fifteen Athens narrative scans, all twelve analytical and Gandhi-era scans, the complete railway-door metaphor, Gandhi's four reform principles, and the dated publication note.

The names மெலிடஸ் and அனிடஸ் are rendered by the standard English forms Meletus and Anytus. ஹேராக்ளியா is rendered Heraclea. பண்டித ஜவஹர் is retained as “Pandit Jawahar,” matching the article's contemporary reference to Jawaharlal Nehru.

எஸ்.ஐ.ஆர். is expanded contextually as the South Indian Railway in the third-class carriage analogy. பண்டா is rendered “panda” and immediately explained, as the source itself does, as a Jagannath temple priest.

The article recounts the reported fates of Meletus and Anytus as part of its 1948 historical and rhetorical argument. These claims are translated as printed rather than silently reconciled with modern historical scholarship.

Sixteen correction events were applied before translation: fifteen scan-backed phrase repairs across the two parts and one established token correction. They restore names, the child's full question, the conspirator summary, Jawahar, and malformed Tamil in the final argument. No period claim or non-material spelling has been modernised in the verbatim source.
